# C4 F3 fix round -- engineer report

Status: all six findings fixed; JVM, Node and Dart lanes green; kondo and
cljstyle clean. No git write commands run.

File set: the six F3 paths. One change of shape:
`test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.clj` (untracked) became
`test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc` so the session scenarios run on
Node and Dart. Its JVM-only tests sit under `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)`, with
`:cljd` first. Nothing else was touched (scratch probes deleted).
`bb gen:python-antlr` was run once to build the ignored parser classes.

## Per finding

1. P1, a blank line submitting an unfinished bracketed expression: FIXED.
   `probe` runs the lexical scan first. A blank line ends a submission only
   when brackets, strings and continuations are all closed.
   Tests: `probe-buffers-across-lines-test`, "a blank line inside brackets
   does not submit" (`print(`, ``, `1)` gives `"" "" "1\n"`, with the
   buffer `"print(\n"` kept), and `probe-classifies-test`
   (`"print(\n"` is :incomplete).

2. P1, triple strings and backslash continuation: FIXED. The regex/count
   heuristic is replaced by `repl-probe/scan`, a char scanner. It tracks
   short and triple strings with escapes (backslash-newline inside a short
   string continues it, raw strings included), `#` comments, typed bracket
   nesting (a mismatched closer is `:error :bracket`), and explicit
   backslash continuation (`:continued`). It also records whether the last
   logical line is indented (`:block?`). This replaces the old "last
   non-blank physical line indented" check, which misread indented
   triple-string content.
   Tests: an open triple string with a `(` inside it, closed on a later
   line (output `"hello\n(\n\n"`); `x = 1 \` then `+ 2` then `print(x)`
   gives `"" "" "3\n"`; `probe-classifies-test` covers the triple-string
   open, close, multiline-indented and bracket-in-content cases, a bracket
   in a string or comment, an escaped quote, a short string continued by a
   backslash, `x = 1 \` versus `x = 1 # \`, and `x = 1 \` + newline +
   `+ 2`.

3. P2, an expected EOF taken for an offending EOF: FIXED, structurally.
   No diagnostic text is matched any more. The probe runs its own parse
   (fresh `parser/make-worker` caches, as before) through a
   `CommonTokenStream` proxy that records the furthest token read. The
   first syntax error is classified by the token it failed on:
   - for a mismatch, the offending token;
   - for a NoViableAltException, the furthest token read when the error
     was reported (measured: ANTLR names the prediction's start token as
     the offending one, e.g. `(` in `print(`).
   The error is mendable (:incomplete) only when that token is EOF or a
   zero-width token after the last token with source text (the NEWLINE
   and DEDENT the lexer synthesizes at end of input). Every other syntax
   error is :complete, even under an open bracket. A lexer error is
   :complete. Measured corpus: `print(`, `print((1,`, `f(a for a in b`,
   `def f(n):`, `@dec` and nested `if x:\n  y = {1: (2` are mendable;
   `x = (1 + * 2`, `foo(1 2`, `x =`, `1 +`, `lambda x:` and `x = )` are
   not.
   Tests: `probe-reports-irreparable-errors-at-once-test` runs eight
   malformed inputs. Each answers :complete, clears `:pending-input`,
   reports SyntaxError, and the next `print(2)` gives `"2\n"`. Also
   `probe-classifies-test`.

4. P1, the `:primed` marker committed by a failed first round: FIXED.
   `lower-parsed` leaves `::frontend {:session s, :display d}` in the
   program's metadata. `eval-compiled` stages it on the round state and
   strips it from whatever comes back. It is committed only by
   `finalize-eval` (via `commit-frontend`), i.e. only when the program
   halted and its VM state survives. A parked require carries it on
   `:pending-run :frontend`, and `recheck-pending*` commits it only on
   completion. Abandoning the require, a link refusal, expansion errors,
   ingestion/append failures, ingress loss and evaluator throws all keep
   the previous `:frontend-session`.
   Tests: `a-failed-first-round-keeps-the-runtime-uninitialized-test`
   (py-report throws once: the marker stays nil, the retry re-runs the
   prelude and `print(x)` gives `"1\n"`), and
   `a-failed-later-round-keeps-the-previous-marker-test` (`:primed` is
   kept and x rolls back to 1).

5. P2, the stray `nil` echo: FIXED with a frontend result-display policy.
   A binding key `:display :output` makes `finalize-eval` show the
   program's output and not its value; the value is still recorded in
   `*1`. Only the ANTLR binding sets it.
   Tests now assert exact responses: assignment `""`, print `"1\n"`,
   `"ZeroDivisionError: division by zero\n"`,
   `"NameError: name 'x' is not defined\n"`, and the block `def` with its
   blank line giving `""`.
   `other-frontends-still-echo-their-value-test` checks that Clojure
   `(+ 1 2)` and legacy Python `1 + 2` still give `"3"`.

6. P2, the catalog test not exercising the parserless id: FIXED.
   `an-explicit-parser-service-makes-an-id-available-test` now selects
   `:yang.python/service` (the bare parserless install). It asserts the
   `unavailable-parser` answer and an unchanged `:clojure` language; the
   pinned-compilation assertion is kept. Added
   `the-antlr-id-is-installed-only-where-its-parser-runs-test`: on the JVM
   it selects `:yang.python/antlr`; on Node and Dart it answers
   unavailable-parser and leaves the language alone (F2 acceptance).

## Added coverage the review listed

- `(vm ...)` rebuild: `vm-rebuild-is-a-new-process-test`. The marker
  clears, the new VM re-runs the prelude, `print(x)` raises NameError,
  then `x = 5` and `print(x)` give `"5\n"`.
- Portable CST-driven session evaluation: every session scenario runs on
