1. P1, a blank line submitting an unfinished bracketed expression: FIXED.
   `probe` runs the lexical scan first. A blank line ends a submission only
   when brackets, strings and continuations are all closed.
   Tests: `probe-buffers-across-lines-test`, "a blank line inside brackets
   does not submit" (`print(`, ``, `1)` gives `"" "" "1\n"`, with the
   buffer `"print(\n"` kept), and `probe-classifies-test`
   (`"print(\n"` is :incomplete).

2. P1, triple strings and backslash continuation: FIXED. The regex/count
   heuristic is replaced by `repl-probe/scan`, a char scanner. It tracks
   short and triple strings with escapes (backslash-newline inside a short
   string continues it, raw strings included), `#` comments, typed bracket
   nesting (a mismatched closer is `:error :bracket`), and explicit
   backslash continuation (`:continued`). It also records whether the last
   logical line is indented (`:block?`). This replaces the old "last
   non-blank physical line indented" check, which misread indented
   triple-string content.
   Tests: an open triple string with a `(` inside it, closed on a later
   line (output `"hello\n(\n\n"`); `x = 1 \` then `+ 2` then `print(x)`
   gives `"" "" "3\n"`; `probe-classifies-test` covers the triple-string
   open, close, multiline-indented and bracket-in-content cases, a bracket
   in a string or comment, an escaped quote, a short string continued by a
   backslash, `x = 1 \` versus `x = 1 # \`, and `x = 1 \` + newline +
   `+ 2`.

3. P2, an expected EOF taken for an offending EOF: FIXED, structurally.
   No diagnostic text is matched any more. The probe runs its own parse
   (fresh `parser/make-worker` caches, as before) through a
   `CommonTokenStream` proxy that records the furthest token read. The
   first syntax error is classified by the token it failed on:
   - for a mismatch, the offending token;
   - for a NoViableAltException, the furthest token read when the error
     was reported (measured: ANTLR names the prediction's start token as
     the offending one, e.g. `(` in `print(`).
   The error is mendable (:incomplete) only when that token is EOF or a
   zero-width token after the last token with source text (the NEWLINE
   and DEDENT the lexer synthesizes at end of input). Every other syntax
   error is :complete, even under an open bracket. A lexer error is
   :complete. Measured corpus: `print(`, `print((1,`, `f(a for a in b`,
   `def f(n):`, `@dec` and nested `if x:\n  y = {1: (2` are mendable;
   `x = (1 + * 2`, `foo(1 2`, `x =`, `1 +`, `lambda x:` and `x = )` are
   not.
   Tests: `probe-reports-irreparable-errors-at-once-test` runs eight
   malformed inputs. Each answers :complete, clears `:pending-input`,
   reports SyntaxError, and the next `print(2)` gives `"2\n"`. Also
   `probe-classifies-test`.

